   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'session_id': '00f0553c-397a-4306-abda-8c7c4b448985', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/perceived-social-support', 'https://elcentro.sonhs.miami.edu/research/measures-library/mspss/index.html', 'https://arc.psych.wisc.edu/self-report/multidimensional-scale-of-perceived-social-support-mspss/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can lead individuals to become overly dependent on others, causing them to feel more stressed when facing challenges independently. As a result, increased social support may actually heighten perceived stress because people worry more about meeting the expectations of their support network.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support can lead individuals to become overly dependent on others, causing them to feel more stressed when facing challenges independently. As a result, increased social support may actually heighten perceived stress because people worry more about meeting the expectations of their support network.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

74. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[POSITIVE]->Prosocial behavior (PGG)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by enhancing individuals' feelings of belonging and trust, making them more likely to engage in cooperative and helpful acts. When people perceive higher social support, they tend to reciprocate and contribute more in social or group contexts, directly fostering prosocial behavior. Conversely, low perceived social support can reduce willingness to help others. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '00f0553c-397a-4306-abda-8c7c4b448985', 'prob_std': 0.29728400109055975, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12076526/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6732855919945194, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by directly improving individuals’ cognitive capacity and decision-making abilities. Higher MSPSS is thought to enhance mental clarity and executive functioning, enabling individuals to more accurately assess and respond to the needs of others in group contexts. As a result, those with greater perceived social support are better equipped to make rational, altruistic choices in social dilemmas, which manifests as increased prosocial behavior (PGG). Conversely, low MSPSS may impair cognitive resources needed for effective prosocial decision-making.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by directly improving individuals’ cognitive capacity and decision-making abilities. Higher MSPSS is thought to enhance mental clarity and executive functioning, enabling individuals to more accurately assess and respo