   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing perceived social support directly reduces loneliness, while decreasing perceived social support increases loneliness. This relationship is direct, widely supported in theory and evidence, and does not appear to be fully mediated by other variables in the provided set.', 'session_id': '00f0553c-397a-4306-abda-8c7c4b448985', 'prob_std': 0.279500834025254, 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12144-015-9337-7', 'https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325434546_Associations_between_loneliness_and_perceived_social_support_and_outcomes_of_mental_health_problems_A_systematic_review', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7760767751482396, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on loneliness (UCLS) primarily because individuals with higher levels of perceived social support are more likely to engage in frequent social activities, which leads to increased social obligations and, paradoxically, less time spent in introspection. This reduction in self-reflection directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness (UCLS), as individuals become distracted from recognizing feelings of isolation. Thus, the inverse relationship between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (UCLS) is driven by the displacement of introspective processes rather than any direct effect of social support on emotional well-being.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) exerts a negative influence on loneliness (UCLS) primarily because individuals with higher levels of perceived social support are more likely to engage in frequent social activities, which leads to increased social obligations and, paradoxically, less time spent in introspection. This reduction in self-reflection directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness (UCLS), as individuals become distracted from recognizing feelings of isolation. Thus, the inverse relationship between perceived social support (MSPSS) and loneliness (UCLS) is driven by the displacement of introspective processes rather than any direct effect of social support on emotional well-being.', 'perplexity': 1.4090002117588092, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8155938334994569, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.01538897804949971, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005002949804000342, 'prob_variance': 0.07812071622081258, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0665909514197354, 'avg_nll': 0.34288038320667324}

