   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased loneliness directly leads to decreased perceived social support, as individuals who feel lonelier tend to perceive less support from others. Conversely, decreased loneliness is causally linked to increased perceived social support.', 'session_id': '00f0553c-397a-4306-abda-8c7c4b448985', 'citations': ['https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12888-018-1736-5', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691822001317', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5104760/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825006286'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Experiencing loneliness can actually make people more aware of their social needs, prompting them to perceive even small acts of kindness as significant support. Therefore, higher loneliness might correlate with higher perceived social support, as individuals become more sensitive to positive social interactions.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing loneliness can actually make people more aware of their social needs, prompting them to perceive even small acts of kindness as significant support. Therefore, higher loneliness might correlate with higher perceived social support, as individuals become more sensitive to positive social interactions.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

