89. Loneliness (UCLS)-[POSITIVE]->Rumination (CERQ-R)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing greater loneliness can lead individuals to engage in more repetitive and negative thinking patterns (rumination), as they attempt to process and cope with their social isolation. Conversely, reducing loneliness tends to decrease rumination by alleviating the need for such coping mechanisms. This relationship is supported by psychological research linking social disconnection to maladaptive cognitive processes.', 'session_id': '00f0553c-397a-4306-abda-8c7c4b448985', 'prob_std': 0.2967684178009476, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s44220-024-00350-x', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3874845/', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/news-room/apa-blogs/rumination-a-cycle-of-negative-thinking', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-96968-7'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7169086894482013, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) contributes to increased Loneliness (UCLS) through its impact on cognitive focus. Individuals who engage in frequent rumination tend to become preoccupied with their own thoughts and internal experiences, which inadvertently leads them to withdraw from social interactions. This inward focus reduces opportunities for meaningful social engagement, thereby increasing feelings of Loneliness (UCLS). Consequently, interventions targeting rumination are likely to directly decrease loneliness by encouraging outward attention and social participation.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) contributes to increased Loneliness (UCLS) through its impact on cognitive focus. Individuals who engage in frequent rumination tend to become preoccupied with their own thoughts and internal experiences, which inadvertently leads them to withdraw from social interactions. This inward focus reduces opportunities for meaningful social engagement, thereby increasing feelings of Loneliness (UCLS). Consequently, interventions targeting rumination are likely to directly decrease loneliness by encouraging outward attention and social participation.', 'perplexity': 1.5255920159359027, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7649003000955124, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04827026308668471, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0039700484728335626, 'prob_variance': 0.08807149380407778, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.146559527518032, 'avg_nll': 0.4223825418975507}

